<h1>Bump Turn Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A bump turn is a right-angle transfer where the product runs off the end of the infeed conveyor into a stop (the "bumper") and is carried away at 90 degrees by a cross conveyor. The product does not rotate in space. It moves sideways, so the face that was a side becomes the leading face. Bump turn modules are a compact, low-cost way to change direction at a conveyor corner in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging and distribution systems</a>, as long as that change in leading face is acceptable downstream.</p>
<!-- VERIFY: "bump turn" is used by some suppliers for any end-stop right-angle transfer and by others more loosely; confirm terminology with supplier literature before quoting a specific product -->
<h2 id="how-heading">How a Bump Turn Works</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Infeed:</strong> The product travels on the infeed conveyor toward the corner.</li>
<li><strong>Stop:</strong> The product's leading face contacts a fixed end stop or side rail at the far side of the transfer. This is the bump.</li>
<li><strong>Cross drive:</strong> A driven surface under the product (belt, powered rollers, O-ring driven rollers, or a gravity decline) pulls the product off at 90 degrees onto the discharge conveyor.</li>
<li><strong>Discharge:</strong> The product leaves with a different face leading. A case that entered long side leading exits short side leading.</li>
</ol>
<p>Because the product is stopped against a rail and then accelerated sideways, a bump turn is a stop-and-go transfer. It suits rigid, flat-bottom cases and totes that tolerate light contact. Fragile, tall, or unstable products may tip or shift on impact.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Bump Turn Module Types</h2>
<h3>Gravity Bump Turns</h3>
<p>On <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller conveyor</a> systems, the infeed and discharge sections are both declined. The product rolls into the end stop, then rolls out the side on rollers set perpendicular to the infeed. No power is used. Gravity bump turns are common in shipping, receiving, and manual pack areas where operators control product spacing.</p>
<h3>Powered Belt or Roller Bump Turns</h3>
<p>On powered lines, the transfer section is a short driven conveyor running at 90 degrees to the infeed. Belt-driven and O-ring-driven roller modules are common in light case handling. <a href="https://www.hytrol.com/products/transfers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol</a>, for example, lists O-ring and flat right angle transfer (F-RAT) models among its transfer products. Powered bump turns keep product moving without relying on infeed momentum, which gives more consistent exit timing.</p>
<h3>Skewed-Roller Bump Turns</h3>
<p>Some modules use rollers skewed toward the side rail so the product is driven against the rail while it turns. The skew keeps the product registered to one edge, which helps downstream scanners and labelers that expect a known edge position.</p>
<h2 id="vs-heading">Bump Turn vs. Curve vs. Pop-Up Transfer vs. Turntable</h2>
<p>Four common ways to change direction by 90 degrees treat product orientation differently. This is the main reason to pick one over another.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Does the Product Rotate in Space?</th>
<th>Leading Face After the Turn</th>
<th>Flow</th>
<th>Main Cost Drivers</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Bump turn</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Changes (side face leads)</td>
<td>Stop-and-go at the bumper</td>
<td>Cross drive, side rail, guarding</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Curve (tapered roller or belt)</td>
<td>Yes, follows the curve</td>
<td>Same face leads</td>
<td>Continuous</td>
<td>Curve radius and floor space, drive</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pop-up chain or belt transfer</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Changes (side face leads)</td>
<td>Stop, lift, transfer, lower</td>
<td>Lift mechanism, controls</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Turntable</td>
<td>Yes, by the table angle</td>
<td>Selectable</td>
<td>Stop, rotate, discharge</td>
<td>Rotation drive, controls, slower cycle</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A 90-degree pop-up transfer, as <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/solutions/pop-up-transfers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner describes it</a>, uses multiple conveyors moving perpendicular to each other to change product direction. Like a bump turn, it moves the product sideways without rotating it. If the same face must keep leading, use a <a href="/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/">curve</a> or a turntable (see <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/pivot/">pivot conveyors</a>).</p>
<h2 id="orientation-heading">Case Orientation Effects</h2>
<p>A 12 x 18 inch case traveling long side leading (18 inch face forward) through a 90-degree bump turn exits with a 12 inch face leading. The case has not rotated, but the conveyor now sees a different face first. This affects downstream equipment.</p>
<h3>When the Change Helps</h3>
<p>Some layouts use a bump turn on purpose. Presenting a different face to a label applicator, changing case orientation before a <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating station</a>, or setting the layer pattern before palletizing can all be done without a dedicated case turner. Two bump turns in sequence return the original leading face.</p>
<h3>When the Change Hurts</h3>
<p>Barcode readers, vision systems, and print-and-apply labelers expect a consistent face and edge. A bump turn before a scanner moves the barcode from one face to another. Plan scanner position and label placement around the turn, or use a curve where the leading face must stay the same.</p>
<h3>Product Behavior</h3>
<p>Rectangular, rigid, flat-bottom products behave predictably at the bumper. Round products (bottles, cans, drums) can spin on contact. Soft packs, polybags, and tall narrow products can deform, tip, or stall. Test samples of your actual product on the module the supplier proposes before committing to the layout.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Bump Turn Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Tight corners in case lines:</strong> A bump turn module turns a line 90 degrees in less floor space than a wide-radius curve. It fits L-shaped layouts around columns and equipment in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Gravity shipping and receiving:</strong> Gravity bump turns route packages from truck doors to staging areas with no power. See <a href="/conveyor-types/flexible-conveyors/">flexible conveyors</a> for portable layouts.</p>
<p><strong>Face presentation for labeling:</strong> A bump turn ahead of a label applicator exposes the target face without a case turner.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center networks:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR systems</a> often use powered right-angle transfer modules at corners where the change in leading face does not matter. Where it does, a powered curve is used instead.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Bump Turn</h2>
<p><strong>Confirm the orientation change is acceptable.</strong> If downstream equipment needs the same leading face, a bump turn is the wrong choice. Use a curve or turntable.</p>
<p><strong>Check product fit.</strong> Rigid, flat-bottom cases and totes suit bump turns. Fragile, tall, round, or soft products need testing or a different method. Give the supplier your smallest and largest product dimensions and weights.</p>
<p><strong>Choose gravity or powered.</strong> Gravity bump turns suit manual, low-rate areas. Powered modules suit automated lines where exit timing must be consistent. Ask the supplier for the rated product weight and rate for the specific module.</p>
<p><strong>Match the rate.</strong> Each product stops at the bumper before leaving, so a bump turn has a maximum rate set by product length, gap, and cross-conveyor speed. Confirm it with the supplier against your peak rate.</p>
<p><strong>Guard the transfer.</strong> The bumper, cross drive, and roller-to-frame gaps create pinch points. Specify guarding per <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1, Safety Standard for Conveyors and Related Equipment</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Keep <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> out of the transfer.</strong> Products queued into a bump turn push against each other and the rail and can jam or skew. Accumulate on straight sections before the transfer.</p>
