<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a bump turn conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A bump turn is a right-angle transfer. The product runs into an end stop at the corner and a cross conveyor carries it away at 90 degrees. The product does not rotate, so a different face leads after the turn. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyor guide</a> for all direction-change methods.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between passive and powered bump turns?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Passive (gravity) bump turns have no motors. Product rolls into the stop and out the side on declined rollers. Powered bump turns use a driven belt or rollers to pull the product off at 90 degrees, which gives more consistent exit timing on automated lines. Passive turns cost less and suit manual, low-rate areas.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Does a bump turn change product orientation?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes, relative to the line. The product does not rotate in space, so the face that was a side becomes the leading face. A pop-up transfer behaves the same way. If the same face must keep leading, use a <a href="/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/">curve</a> or a turntable.</p>

</div>
</div>
